From 306038d21e1f9bff29497359a6e86022a6095402 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:30:37 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 10/19] feat(core): add resources_rollout: restart, undo,
 status (C10)

Restarting or rolling back a workload meant re-applying it: job 3 of the
native session changed a template annotation by a full re-apply of a
Deployment (1,208 tokens) to restart it, job 4 did the same to roll
back, and job 19 listed ReplicaSets, read the previous one and
re-applied the whole Deployment spec from it.

resources_rollout(action, kind, namespace, name, to_revision), for
apps/v1 Deployment, StatefulSet and DaemonSet:
- restart: sets kubectl.kubernetes.io/restartedAt on the Pod template
  (a strategic merge patch, as kubectl rollout restart does), so the
  controller replaces the Pods with its own rollout strategy;
- undo (Deployments): finds the ReplicaSets the Deployment owns, picks
  the previous revision (or to_revision), and replaces the Deployment's
  Pod template with that ReplicaSet's template, minus its
  pod-template-hash label, as kubectl rollout undo does. The patch is
  guarded by a resourceVersion test, so a concurrent change fails it
  instead of being overwritten. StatefulSet and DaemonSet history lives
  in ControllerRevisions and is not handled (the error says so);
- status: generation and observed generation, desired, updated, ready
  and available replicas, the revision and the conditions, and whether
  the rollout is complete.

A closed upstream PR (#1294) proposed separate rollout_status,
rollout_history, rollout_undo and rollout_restart tools; this is one
tool with an action argument, the pattern of vm_lifecycle and
tekton_pipelinerun_lifecycle. Annotated destructive, not idempotent.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
